    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Contributing guidelines\n\n- [x] I have read [CONTRIBUTING.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md)\n- [x] I have read [CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/main/CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md)\n\n### Module(s)\n\nmini.statusline\n\n### Description\n\nI'll start by saying that the simplicity of `mini.statusline` compared to other statusline plugins is impressive.\n\nI would like to suggest a small feature that can be implemented as a user-facing function. It can also be a \"show and tell\" kind of thing, so it can be shown here or in the discussion.\n\nThis feature will help in displaying or hiding groups based on a priority value rather than hard-coded truncation width values. This can be useful if don't know what is a good value for when a component should be truncated or when it can dynamically change and you want an easier to configure their visibility. It may also be helpful if you are using multiple screens of different resolutions.\n\nFor example, sometimes I don't care about line count info or git diff info or git branch but I would want LSP diagnostics error and warning count and filename.\n\nSimple implementation details:\n1. To the group we add two new keys apart from \"strings\" and \"hl\", which are \"string_cb\" (can also be \"cb\", \"callback\", \"fn\") and \"priority\".\n2. We use a new function similar to `MiniStatusline.combine_groups`, say `sort_groups` which takes the groups and sorts them based on the priority in descending order.\n3. We export the sorted groups so that we only sort once and for subsequent calls use this exported one\n4. We will go through each group in a loop and do the following:\n    1. (before starting the loop create a variable for statusline with initial value empty string and a variable for statusline width with initial value 0)\n    2. create the string using \"string_cb\".\n    3. Calculate the string evaluation width using `vim.api.nvim_eval_statusline(str, {winid=0, maxwidth=0}).width` or similar\n    4. Check `if` this width + existing statusline width > `vim.api.nvim_win_get_width(0)` and `break`\n    5. `else` add the string to the status line string and increment the statusline width by that value\n\nThis way the groups can auto-hide or show based on the window width",
