            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\n\nI appreciate the step-by-step explanation of the idea! Although, thorough, there is one key issue with it.\n\nOne of the design goals for 'mini.statusline' module is to be *extremely* fast for the actual content execution. This is important because statusline gets evaluated *very* frequently (slightly more frequently than on every key press). For the \"accumulate content string as you go through ordered sections\" approach to work it indeed needs a `vim.api.nvim_eval_statusline()` call on every iteration, there is no other way. This is too costly as it will basically at least double all needed computations for the final display string (in reality probably more as `nvim_eval_statusline()` itself must have an overhead).\n\nAnother issue here comes when reaching the width limit after going through sections ordered by priority. As initial order of sections might be not the same as by decreased priority, there would have to be another sort (or some clever/tedious book-keeping) of the sections to be in their \"display order\". This is also costly.\n\n---\n\nAll in all, I am pretty confident that this approach will not be built-in in 'mini.statusline'. Having the ability to cut off based on the window width is usually enough for adjusting content. It can indeed be seen as a proxy for the \"priority\" to a certain extent: the bigger the cutoff width, the less priority the section has (as the short output will be returned earlier).\n\nThe 'mini.statusline' approach with allowing any function content (with [this](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/4630b9cb783b280fa0fd94e8245d8fb06023f3ba/doc/mini-statusline.txt#L86-L106) being default for active content) was chosen exactly to allow this kind of flexibility for users. I'd encourage you to implement the idea yourself, see if it lives up to your expectations, and then post it as a [\"Show and tell\" discussion](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/discussions/categories/show-and-tell).",
