        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "> Another issue here comes when reaching the width limit after going through sections ordered by priority. As initial order of sections might be not the same as by decreased priority, there would have to be another sort (or some clever/tedious book-keeping) of the sections to be in their \"display order\". This is also costly.\n\nThis is solved by the third point in my simple impl details.\n\n> All in all, I am pretty confident that this approach will not be built-in in 'mini.statusline'. Having the ability to cut off based on the window width is usually enough for adjusting content. It can indeed be seen as a proxy for the \"priority\" to a certain extent: the bigger the cutoff width, the less priority the section has (as the short output will be returned earlier).\n\nThis can be true but the main problem here is hiding one section can give space for other section. It's rather inconvenient to find out these truncation values for each component which would still fail if the string generated varies.\n\n> One of the design goals for 'mini.statusline' module is to be _extremely_ fast for the actual content execution. This is important because statusline gets evaluated _very_ frequently (slightly more frequently than on every key press). For the \"accumulate content string as you go through ordered sections\" approach to work it indeed needs a `vim.api.nvim_eval_statusline()` call on every iteration, there is no other way. This is too costly as it will basically at least double all needed computations for the final display string (in reality probably more as `nvim_eval_statusline()` itself must have an overhead).\n\nI can see this being an issue. Let me implement this and see if it actually causes an issue in practice. Do you think I have to call `nvim_eval_statusline()` when I am using something that returns a non-special string?",
