        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> This is solved by the third point in my simple impl details.\n\nI don't think it is. It sorts groups by priority ones, yes. But each 'statusline' evaluation might lead to different set of groups being displayed. The order in which they should be displayed might be different. As they were traversed in \"priority order\", there needs to be a way to convert them to \"display order\".\n\n> I can see this being an issue. Let me implement this and see if it actually causes an issue in practice.\n\nOh, in practice this would probably be unnoticed, as we are talking about ~1us (microsecond) per `nvim_eval_statusline()` call while default content takes ~155us on my machine. Yet it would still be visible in benchmarks to the point that I don't think it is worth it (also taking added complexity into account).\n\n> Do you think I have to call `nvim_eval_statusline()` when I am using something that returns a non-special string?\n\nProbably not and using something `vim.fn.strdisplaywidth()` would be enough. Not sure if it will be much faster, though.",
